     "answer": "The human heart is a muscular organ about the size of a fist located in the chest cavity between the lungs. It has four chambers: two upper atria (right and left) and two lower ventricles (right and left). The heart is divided into two sides by a septum. The right side receives deoxygenated blood from the body and pumps it to the lungs, while the left side receives oxygenated blood from the lungs and pumps it to the body. The heart has four valves: tricuspid, pulmonary, mitral, and aortic, which ensure one-way flow of blood. The heart functions through a cardiac cycle consisting of systole (contraction) and diastole (relaxation). During systole, the ventricles contract and pump blood out, while during diastole, the atria contract and fill the ventricles with blood. The heart's pumping action is regulated by electrical impulses generated by the sinoatrial node (pacemaker) and conducted through the heart muscle."
