     "question": "Explain the structure and function of the human heart.",
     "marks": 5,
     "type": "long",
     "probability": "very-high",
     "answer": "The human heart is a muscular organ about the size of a fist that pumps blood throughout the body. It has four chambers: two upper atria and two lower ventricles. The right atrium receives deoxygenated blood from the body through the vena cava and pumps it to the right ventricle. The right ventricle pumps this blood to the lungs through the pulmonary artery for oxygenation. The left atrium receives oxygenated blood from the lungs through the pulmonary veins and pumps it to the left ventricle. The left ventricle, being the most muscular chamber, pumps oxygenated blood to the rest of the body through the aorta. The heart has valves between the chambers and at the exits of the ventricles to ensure one-way blood flow. The coronary arteries supply blood to the heart muscle itself. The heart's rhythmic contractions are controlled by electrical impulses generated by the sinoatrial node, making it an efficient double pump system that maintains circulation."
