     "answer": "Electrolytic refining of copper is a process used to purify impure copper. The setup consists of an electrolyte containing copper sulfate (CuSO4) solution. A thick block of impure copper is made the anode, and a thin strip of pure copper is made the cathode. When electric current is passed, copper atoms from the impure anode lose electrons and dissolve into the solution as Cu2+ ions: Cu (impure) -> Cu2+ + 2e-. These Cu2+ ions then move to the cathode where they gain electrons and deposit as pure copper: Cu2+ + 2e- -> Cu (pure). Impurities like gold and silver settle below the anode as anode mud. This process yields highly pure copper (99.99%) suitable for electrical applications."
