     "answer": "The human heart is a muscular organ about the size of a fist located in the chest cavity between the lungs. It has four chambers: two atria (upper chambers) and two ventricles (lower chambers). The right atrium receives deoxygenated blood from the body through the vena cava, which then flows to the right ventricle. The right ventricle pumps this blood to the lungs through the pulmonary artery for oxygenation. The left atrium receives oxygenated blood from the lungs through the pulmonary veins, which then flows to the left ventricle. The left ventricle pumps this oxygenated blood to the rest of the body through the aorta. The heart has four valves: tricuspid, pulmonary, mitral, and aortic, which ensure unidirectional blood flow. The heart's rhythmic contractions (heartbeat) are controlled by electrical impulses generated by the sinoatrial node (SA node), making it an efficient pump that circulates blood throughout the body."
