     "para": "A reflex action is an involuntary and nearly instantaneous movement in response to a stimulus. The pathway taken by nerve impulses in a reflex action is called a reflex arc. A typical reflex arc consists of a receptor, sensory neuron, relay neuron in the spinal cord, motor neuron, and effector. Reflex actions are protective in nature and do not involve the brain, making them faster than voluntary actions. The knee jerk reflex is an example of a monosynaptic reflex involving only two neurons. Reflexes can be classified as somatic (involving skeletal muscles) or autonomic (involving smooth muscles and glands). The reflex arc ensures quick responses to potentially harmful stimuli.",
     "points": [
      "Reflex actions are involuntary responses to stimuli that protect the body from harm",
      "The reflex arc is the neural pathway that mediates a reflex action",
      "Reflex arcs do not always involve the brain, allowing for faster responses",
      "Receptors detect stimuli and send signals via sensory neurons",
      "Relay neurons in the spinal cord process the information",
      "Motor neurons transmit signals to effectors that respond to the stimulus",
      "The knee jerk reflex is a simple reflex involving only two neurons",
      "Reflex actions can be somatic or autonomic depending on the effector"
     ],
