     "answer": "Double fertilization is a unique process in flowering plants where two fertilization events occur in the embryo sac. After pollination, the pollen grain germinates on the stigma to form a pollen tube which grows through the style to reach the ovule. The pollen tube enters the embryo sac through the micropyle. The embryo sac contains an egg cell, two synergids, three antipodal cells, and two polar nuclei. One male gamete fuses with the egg cell to form the zygote which develops into the embryo. This is the first fertilization event. The second male gamete fuses with the two polar nuclei to form the primary endosperm cell which develops into the endosperm that provides nutrition to the developing embryo. This is the second fertilization event. Since two fertilization events occur, it is called double fertilization. The zygote and the primary endosperm cell are both products of fertilization, hence the name."
