     "question": "What is corrosion? Explain the rusting of iron and methods to prevent it.",
     "marks": 5,
     "type": "long",
     "probability": "very-high",
     "answer": "Corrosion is the gradual destruction of materials (usually metals) by chemical reaction with their environment. The rusting of iron is a common example of corrosion. When iron is exposed to oxygen and moisture, it undergoes oxidation to form hydrated iron(III) oxide (rust). The chemical reaction is: 4Fe + 3O2 + xH2O -> 2Fe2O3·xH2O. This process is accelerated by impurities, water, and electrolytes. Methods to prevent corrosion include: 1. Galvanization: Coating iron with a layer of zinc, which is more reactive and acts as a sacrificial anode. 2. Painting: Applying a protective coating to prevent contact with oxygen and moisture. 3. Alloying: Mixing iron with other metals to form stainless steel, which is more resistant to corrosion. 4. Oil greasing: Creating a barrier to prevent moisture contact. 5. Cathodic protection: Using a more reactive metal to protect iron from oxidation."
