     "question": "Explain the extraction of aluminum from bauxite.",
     "marks": 3,
     "type": "long",
     "probability": "high",
     "answer": "Aluminum is extracted from its ore, bauxite (Al2O3·2H2O), through a process called the Bayer process followed by electrolysis. First, bauxite is purified to obtain alumina (Al2O3) through the Bayer process: Bauxite is digested in hot sodium hydroxide solution to dissolve aluminum as sodium aluminate. The impurities (iron oxide, silica) remain as insoluble residues. The sodium aluminate solution is then seeded with crystals to precipitate aluminum hydroxide. The precipitate is filtered, washed, and calcined to produce pure alumina. The purified alumina is then dissolved in molten cryolite (Na3AlF6) to lower its melting point. The mixture is electrolyzed in a steel tank lined with graphite. The graphite acts as the cathode, and graphite rods act as the anodes. During electrolysis, aluminum ions are reduced at the cathode to form molten aluminum: Al3+ + 3e- -> Al. Oxide ions are oxidized at the anode to form oxygen gas: 2O2- -> O2 + 4e-. The molten aluminum sinks to the bottom and is tapped off periodically."
