     "answer": "Excretion is the process of eliminating metabolic waste products from the body. In humans, the primary excretory organs are the kidneys, which filter blood to form urine. The kidneys contain numerous filtering units called nephrons. Each nephron consists of a renal corpuscle (containing Bowman's capsule and a glomerulus) and a renal tubule. Blood enters the glomerulus under high pressure, and water, salts, glucose, urea, and other small molecules are filtered into Bowman's capsule, forming glomerular filtrate. Large molecules like proteins and blood cells remain in the blood. The filtrate then passes through the proximal convoluted tubule, where most of the glucose, amino acids, and salts are reabsorbed into the blood. The filtrate then enters the loop of Henle, where water is reabsorbed, creating a concentration gradient. The distal convoluted tubule further adjusts the composition of the filtrate by reabsorbing or secreting substances as needed. Finally, the filtrate passes through the collecting duct, where more water is reabsorbed, forming urine. The urine is then transported to the bladder through the ureters and stored until it is excreted through the urethra. Other excretory organs include the lungs (which expel carbon dioxide), the skin (which excretes sweat containing water, salts, and urea), and the liver (which converts ammonia to urea)."
