     "heading": "Force on a Current-Carrying Conductor in a Magnetic Field",
     "para": "When a current-carrying conductor is placed in a magnetic field, it experiences a force. The magnitude of this force depends on the strength of the current, the length of the conductor in the magnetic field, the strength of the magnetic field, and the angle between the conductor and the field. The force is maximum when the conductor is perpendicular to the magnetic field and zero when it is parallel. The direction of the force can be determined using Fleming's left-hand rule. According to this rule, if the first finger points in the direction of the magnetic field, the middle finger in the direction of current, then the thumb will point in the direction of the force experienced by the conductor. This principle is used in electric motors and other devices that convert electrical energy into mechanical energy.",
