     "heading": "Transformer",
     "para": "A transformer is a device that changes the voltage of an alternating current without changing its frequency. It consists of two coils called primary and secondary wound on a common soft iron core. When an alternating current flows through the primary coil, it creates a changing magnetic field in the core, which induces an alternating current in the secondary coil. A step-up transformer increases the voltage from primary to secondary, while a step-down transformer decreases it. The voltage transformation ratio is equal to the turns ratio of the coils (Vs/Vp = Ns/Np). Transformers work on the principle of electromagnetic induction and are used in power transmission to reduce energy losses. The efficiency of a transformer is given by the ratio of output power to input power. Ideal transformers have 100% efficiency, but practical transformers have some losses due to resistance in coils, hysteresis in the core, and eddy currents.",
