     "question": "What are soaps and detergents? Explain their cleansing action.",
     "marks": 3,
     "type": "short",
     "probability": "high",
     "answer": "Soaps are sodium or potassium salts of long-chain carboxylic acids (fatty acids). They are made by the saponification reaction between fats or oils and an alkali like sodium hydroxide. Detergents are ammonium or sulphonate salts of long-chain hydrocarbons. They are synthetic cleansing agents. Both soaps and detergents have a hydrophilic (water-attracting) head and a hydrophobic (water-repelling) tail. When used for cleaning, the hydrophobic tails attach to dirt or grease particles, while the hydrophilic heads remain in water. This forms micelles where the dirt particles are trapped in the center, surrounded by the hydrophobic tails. The micelles are then washed away with water, effectively removing the dirt from the surface."
