     "question": "Describe the structure and function of the human heart.",
     "marks": 5,
     "type": "long",
     "probability": "high",
     "answer": "The human heart is a muscular organ about the size of a fist, located in the chest cavity between the lungs. It has four chambers: two upper atria and two lower ventricles. The right atrium receives deoxygenated blood from the body through the vena cava, which then flows into the right ventricle. The right ventricle pumps this blood to the lungs through the pulmonary artery for oxygenation. The left atrium receives oxygenated blood from the lungs through the pulmonary veins, which then flows into the left ventricle. The left ventricle pumps this oxygenated blood to the rest of the body through the aorta. The heart has valves (tricuspid, pulmonary, mitral, aortic) that ensure one-way flow of blood. The heart's pumping action is due to the rhythmic contraction (systole) and relaxation (diastole) of its muscles, which is regulated by the sinoatrial node (natural pacemaker)."
