     "probability": "very-high",
     "answer": "Excretion is the process of eliminating metabolic waste products from the body. In humans, the primary excretory organ is the kidney. Each kidney contains numerous nephrons, which are the functional units. The nephron filters blood under high pressure in the glomerulus. The filtrate contains water, glucose, salts, urea, and other wastes. As this filtrate passes through the tubule, useful substances like glucose and salts are reabsorbed. The remaining fluid, now containing concentrated wastes, forms urine. Urine flows through the ureters to the bladder, where it is stored before being expelled through the urethra. Other excretory organs include the skin (sweat), lungs (carbon dioxide), and liver (bile pigments)."
